d.\n- A camera positioned behind Benny, looking forward.\n\nThe previous game looked too pale, with oversized orange spiral rings, an incorrectly shaped tube, and a narrow water channel.\n\nFix those differences precisely.\n\nHave the Environment Modeler, Water FX Specialist, Camera Engineer, Lighting/Shader specialists, and Playwright Visual QA Specialist work together.\n\nRender new screenshots from the real running game.\n\nCompare the new results directly against my original approved Screen 05 image.\n\nProvide separate full-size before-and-after images and report the remaining differences.\n\n6. PRESERVE THE ENTIRE ELEVEN-SCREEN GAME\n\nMy approved story sequence must remain:\n\n01 — INTO THE WASH\n\n02 — WHIRLPOOL TUMBLE\n\n03 — WHIRLPOOL TWIRL\n\n04 — THROUGH THE FIVE ARROWS\n\n05 — FLOW INTO THE CURRENT\n\n06 — CREST THE RINSE ARCH\n\n07 — DIVE THE WASHER TUBE\n\n08 — FINAL SPIN CHAMBER\n\n09 — RIDE THE FINAL WAVE\n\n10 — SLIDE INTO HIDING\n\n11 — SAFELY HOME\n\nAll environments must belong to one continuous, believable washing-machine adventure.\n\nThe previously verified Screen 07 to Screen 08 descent was 61 game units.\n\nPreserve that continuous deep descent without teleportation, gaps, or sudden camera jumps.\n\nKeep the gameplay interactive rather than using static images or videos for the swimming sections.\n\n7. PRESERVE BENNY AND MY OFFICIAL CARTOON GIRL\n\nBenny must remain the same approved fluffy pink mascot with purple eyes, pink paw pads, and the purple-and-white striped beanie with a large pom-pom and gold star.\n\nMy official cartoon girl must have:\n\n- Curly brown ponytail.\n- Purple bow headband with gold star.\n- Gold hoop earrings.\n- Large expressive cartoon eyes.\n- Purple-and-white striped hoodie.\n- Blue jeans with a yellow star patch.\n- Purple sneakers with gold star details.\n\nDo not replace her with a realistic-looking woman.\n\nThe previous GLB characters used part-based animation rather than full skeletal rigs.\n\nHave the Character Artists assess proper rigging and animations without changing the approved character appearances.\n\nPreserve existing animations until replacements are verified.\n\n8. GOOGLE FLOW IS HANDLING THE INTRODUCTION\n\nI am creating my Rainbow opening cinematic using Google Flow.\n\nThe girl gently puts the purple towel containing Benny inside the pink-and-gold washing machine, closes the door, and walks away.\n\nThe video is short, natural-looking, and completely silent.\n\nMy project already has some Google Flow video integration support.\n\nRestore and inspect that existing code.\n\nPrepare the app so I can provide the final Google Flow MP4 and integrate it without rebuilding the game.\n\nThe sequence must be:\n\nGoogle Flow opening cinematic → washer transition → playable 3D gameplay → final Safely Home ending.\n\nKeep Benny concealed from the girl.\n\nThe same official cartoon girl appears in the ending.\n\nDo not invent a completed MP4 if it has not been added to the repository. Ask me to upload the actual video when needed.\n\n9. ALL ANDROID SCREENS MUST FIT EDGE TO EDGE\n\nMy game must fill the entire display of different Android phones.\n\nUse portrait orientation with a 9:16 reference composition, but properly accommodate other Android phone aspect ratios.\n\nDo not create black bars, white borders, or empty side areas.\n\nExtend the 3D scenery to the edges.\n\nUse safe areas for the HUD, camera, Benny, and other important content.\n\nDo not stretch the characters or scenery.\n\nUse responsive camera framing and appropriate video cover/cropping behavior.\n\nTest multiple Android portrait aspect ratios using Playwright.\n\nEnsure the image and background remain visually complete across different devices.\n\n10. RESTORE THE FIVE-MINUTE GITHUB AUTOSAVE\n\nMy previous Claude account had automatic five-minute backups.\n\nRestore the backup scripts, session hooks, and branch configuration.\n\nConfigure the backup destination for the new working branch, protecting the original branch.\n\nAutomatically save and push changes every five minutes while the development environment is active.\n\nAlso perform a safe final backup when a session or development task finishes.\n\nNever force-push or overwrite the original backup.\n\nDo not save passwords, access tokens, or API keys into GitHub.\n\nVerify remote commits and report failures.\n\nIf the cloud container shuts down, explain that the timer may stop. Reinitialize the supported backup mechanism when a new session starts.\n\nPreserve the previous session transcripts, specialist logs, and current state documents.\n\n11. UPDATE AND MAINTAIN THE RECOVERY DOCUMENTS\n\nRead the saved recovery and project handoff documents.\n\nUpdate:\n\n\"backups/CURRENT_STATE.md\"\n\n\"backups/AGENTS_STATUS.md\"\n\n\"backups/RECOVERY.md\"\n\nAlso check the saved QA documentation and Android build instructions.\n\nRecord:\n\n- Current GitHub branch and latest verified commit.\n- Restored tools and their actual connection status.\n- All ten specialists and their work status.\n- Screen 05 improvements and remaining problems.\n- Android build status.\n- Google Flow integration status.\n- World Labs access or credit requirements.\n- The next development tasks.\n\nPreserve the project history so a future Claude account or session can continue without me having to explain everything again.\n\n12. FIRST ACTIONS — DO THESE IN ORDER\n\nFIRST: Verify the correct private repository and latest remote commit on my recovery branch.\n\nSECOND: Inspect the repository state, fetch Git LFS assets, and verify all reference images and source files.\n\nTHIRD: Restore the ten agents and read the previous conversation and development handoff logs.\n\nFOURTH: Verify the development environment, tool connections, and Android build.\n\nFIFTH: Restore and verify the five-minute backup configuration, protecting the original branch.\n\nSIXTH: Identify the exact unfinished Screen 05 work and resume the specialists from their saved progress.\n\nSEVENTH: Produce real mobile gameplay screenshots and compare them with my approved artwork.\n\nEIGHTH: Continue improving Screens 01–11 while maintaining the complete connected gameplay route.\n\nDo not stop after writing a plan.\n\nBegin the actual project recovery and development.\n\n13. WHAT I WANT YOU TO REPORT\n\nBefore spending significant development credits, briefly show me:\n\n1. The actual GitHub repository and latest recovery commit.\n2. The working branch.\n3. How many files and assets were successfully restored.\n4. Whether all ten specialist agents loaded.\n5. Which tools and MCP servers are connected, unavailable, or awaiting login.\n6. Whether the Android game builds.\n7. Whether the five-minute autosave successfully pushed a test commit when appropriate.\n8. Which Screen 05 tasks remain unfinished.\n\nAfter verifying recovery, continue implementing the unfinished work.\n\nMy goal is to use the credits available on THIS Claude account to continue EXACTLY where my previous Claude account stopped.\n\nI do not want another prototype, a different game, recreated agents, or redesigned artwork.\n\nI want my original Rainbow game restored, protected, and improved using all the saved work.\n\nSTART NOW.", "operation": "enqueue", "sessionId": "eae80a1c-be41-5f1a-858b-469afcc249da", "timestamp": "2026-11-15T19:10:09.275Z", "type": "queue-operation"}
