AME\n\nMy previous Claude reported approximately 298 saved project files.\n\nRecover and verify all available files, including:\n\n- All 11 approved Rainbow screen references, in their exact order.\n- The official cartoon girl reference image.\n- Benny's 3D GLB model, source code, textures, and animations.\n- The cartoon girl's GLB model, source code, textures, and animations.\n- All washing-machine environment geometry.\n- All metallic tunnels, gold rings, washer interiors, and machinery.\n- All animated cyan-and-pink water systems.\n- Foam, bubbles, splashes, shaders, and lighting.\n- Gameplay path, swimming movement, camera, controls, coins, hearts, and HUD.\n- The Android Capacitor application and available APK.\n- All Playwright screenshots, reference comparisons, and visual QA tests.\n- All saved Blender scripts and available 3D source files.\n- The World Labs Screen 05 pilot package.\n- All prompts, reference-matching instructions, recovery documents, and project history.\n- The previous Claude conversation transcripts and specialist logs that are actually stored in GitHub.\n- The automatic backup scripts, configuration files, and session hooks.\n\nRun \"git lfs pull\" after cloning or fetching the project so the real models and other large files are restored rather than leaving only Git LFS pointer files.\n\nVerify the actual contents and integrity of important binary assets.\n\nDo not assume a file is restored merely because its filename exists.\n\n3. RESTORE ALL TEN SPECIALIST DESIGN AGENTS\n\nMy project already has ten specialist agents saved in:\n\n\".claude/agents/\"\n\nRestore and verify every one:\n\n1. Reference Match Director\n2. Benny Character Artist\n3. Cartoon Girl Character Artist\n4. Washer Environment Modeler\n5. Water FX Specialist\n6. Foam and Bubble Specialist\n7. Path and Camera Transition Engineer\n8. Animation and Gameplay Engineer\n9. Playwright Visual QA Specialist\n10. Android Build and Performance Specialist\n\nDo not create new replacement agents if my existing ones are recoverable.\n\nRead their actual saved instructions, responsibilities, work logs, and latest completed tasks.\n\nRestore their project context and reinitialize the agents needed for the unfinished work.\n\nI understand that live agent processes cannot simply be transferred between Claude accounts. Restart the specialists from their saved configurations, previous outputs, and progress notes.\n\nPreserve their completed work.\n\nCoordinate the specialists so they can improve the game together instead of duplicating or overwriting one another's work.\n\n4. RESTORE ALL DEVELOPMENT TOOLS, SERVERS, AND PLUGINS\n\nInspect the saved project configuration and recover or reconnect the supported tools.\n\nBLENDER\n\nMy previous environment used Blender 5.3.0.\n\nVerify whether Blender is available in this new Claude environment.\n\nUse Blender Python scripting for automated 3D modeling and rendering.\n\nIf Blender MCP is available and working, use it where appropriate.\n\nIf the desktop Blender MCP requires a graphical window unavailable in this cloud environment, continue with headless Blender and Python scripts instead.\n\nDo not falsely report Blender MCP as connected if it is not.\n\nUse Blender to improve the washer geometry, tunnel shapes, metallic materials, character models, and visual accuracy.\n\nWORLD LABS MARBLE / ATLAS\n\nRestore the existing official World Labs plugin and its available MCP configuration.\n\nMy previous Claude environment had the official World Labs plugin installed and an Atlas connector configured.\n\nVerify whether World Labs authentication is available in this new account.\n\nIf my Atlas account has not been activated, report the account-access problem and continue with the other available tools.\n\nDo not demand an API key if a supported OAuth sign-in can be used.\n\nUse World Labs to help reconstruct my exact approved Rainbow environments when the account and tools are ready.\n\nDo not generate unrelated or redesigned environments.\n\nDo not spend paid World Labs credits until I explicitly approve the cost.\n\nPLAYWRIGHT\n\nRestore Playwright and the mobile screenshot testing workflow.\n\nTest the actual running 3D game in Android-style portrait viewports.\n\nRender screenshots, compare them with the approved originals, and identify visual mismatches.\n\nOTHER 3D ASSET TOOLS\n\nCheck whether Meshy MCP and Hyper3D Rodin MCP are available.\n\nIf not connected, report that clearly and explain which account connection or authorization is required.\n\nDo not spend paid asset-generation credits without my approval.\n\nANDROID\n\nRestore the Android SDK and build environment.\n\nThe previous environment used Android SDK Platform 34.\n\nCheck the existing setup scripts and install the required dependencies using the supported setup method.\n\nVerify TypeScript, the production web build, the Capacitor Android build, and the existing gameplay tests.\n\nDo not report a successful phone test unless the app was actually tested on a device or emulator.\n\n5. RESUME THE UNFINISHED SCREEN 05 SPECIALIST WORK FIRST\n\nTHIS IS MY MOST IMPORTANT UNFINISHED TASK.\n\nWhen my previous Claude account ran out of usage credits, five specialists were improving:\n\nSCREEN 05 — FLOW INTO THE CURRENT\n\nThe previous Claude reported that these specialists were still editing files and that their final before-and-after screenshots were not yet completed.\n\nStart by inspecting the latest saved Screen 05 code, agent transcripts, progress notes, and visual comparisons.\n\nDetermine which specialist changes were actually saved to GitHub and which remained unfinished.\n\nDo not undo completed improvements.\n\nContinue correcting Screen 05 so it matches my approved reference.\n\nMy approved Screen 05 has:\n\n- A deep magenta metallic washer tube.\n- Evenly spaced reflective yellow-gold rings.\n- Correctly sized perforations in the tube walls.\n- A wide, beautiful, winding cyan-and-hot-pink water current.\n- Thick moving water with white foam and bubbles.\n- Gold dollar tokens following the natural water path.\n- Benny correctly sized in the lower foreground.\n- A camera positioned behind Benny, looking forward.\n\nThe previous game looked too pale, with oversized orange spiral rings, an incorrectly shaped tube, and a narrow water channel.\n\nFix those differences precisely.\n\nHave the Environment Modeler, Water FX Specialist, Camera Engineer, Lighting/Shader specialists, and Playwright Visual QA Specialist work together.\n\nRender new screenshots from the real running game.\n\nCompare the new results directly against my original approved Screen 05 image.\n\nProvide separate full-size before-and-after images and report the remaining differences.\n\n6. PRESERVE THE ENTIRE ELEVEN-SCREEN GAME\n\nMy approved story sequence must remain:\n\n01 — INTO THE WASH\n\n02 — WHIRLPOOL TUMBLE\n\n03 — WHIRLPOOL TWIRL\n\n04 — THROUGH THE FIVE ARROWS\n\n05 — FLOW INTO THE CURRENT\n\n06 — CREST THE RINSE ARCH\n\n07 — DIVE THE WASHER TUBE\n\n08 — FINAL SPIN CHAMBER\n\n09 — RIDE THE FINAL WAVE\n\n10 — SLIDE INTO HIDING\n\n11 — SAFELY HOME\n\nAll environments must belong to one continuous, believable washing-machine adventure.\n\nThe previously verified Screen 07 to Screen 08 descent was 61 game units.\n\nPreserve that continuous deep descent without teleportation, gaps, or sudden camera jumps.\n\nKeep the gameplay interactive rather than using static images or videos for the swimming sections.\n\n7. PRESERVE BENNY AND MY OFFICIAL CARTOON GIRL\n\nBenny must remain the same approved fluffy pink mascot with purple eyes, pink paw pads, and the purple-and-white striped beanie with a large pom-pom and gold star.\n\nMy official cartoon girl must have:\n\n- Curly brown ponytail.\n- Purple bow headband with gold star.\n- Gold hoop earrings.\n- Large expressive cartoon eyes.\n- Purple-and-white striped hoodie.\n- Blue jeans with a yellow star patch.\n- Purple sneakers with gold star details